    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> port that won self-government from its archbishops after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>'s privilege of 1186.</li><li>A Hanseatic city and free imperial city, it resisted Swedish claims and survived the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1806.</li><li>Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremerhaven">Bremerhaven</a> it became a great emigration port, and it is now the smallest state of the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen-ratskeller">Ratskeller</a> wine cellar beneath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>'s town hall, in use since the early fifteenth century, still keeps a cask of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudesheim-am-rhein">Rüdesheim</a> wine from 1653.</p></aside>
    </section>

